#use(std/code)

#section{Source Formatting}::

The CLI doubles as a conservative, semantics-preserving formatter for
#code{.yamd} sources. It parses the original and every candidate rewrite with
the real reader and accepts a change only if the two read to equivalent
documents — correctness never depends on line heuristics

#code(lang: "plain")
	racket -l- yamd/cli --fmt file.yamd            # rewrite in place
	racket -l- yamd/cli --fmt a.yamd b.yamd c.yamd # several files at once
	racket -l- yamd/cli --fmt file.yamd -o out.yamd
	racket -l- yamd/cli --fmt --check file.yamd    # exit 1 if not formatted (CI)

#code{--check} over several files reports every unformatted file before
exiting 1; #code{-o} requires exactly one input. Repo-integration recipes
(dev shell, git hook, CI) live in docs/usage/formatter.md

What it normalizes:

#list
	#item: CRLF → LF; whitespace-only lines emptied
	#item: Runs of 3+ blank lines → 2, outside verbatim regions (raw blocks, fences, multi-line inline bodies are protected)
	#item
		A final newline is guaranteed; a trailing run of blank lines collapses to one blank line — never zero, because a
		trailing blank is a real paragraph separator when the file is spliced mid-flow by #code{#include}
	#item: The space after a leading call's #code{:} body introducer → exactly one space

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		Trailing whitespace on content lines is #strong{kept} — in YAMD a text line's trailing spaces are literal output,
		so the usual strip-trailing-whitespace rule would change the render
	#item: Documents that do not parse are reported and never rewritten
	#item: #code{--fmt --check} writes nothing; plain #code{--fmt} rewrites in place unless #code{-o} redirects
	#item
		Blank-line collapses inside a multi-line custom body (e.g. #code{#math}) are only applied when provably
		render-neutral; otherwise that document's interior blanks are left alone
